embership.\\n  - risk_perception: float in [0,1]; intrinsic propensity to wear masks given perceived risk.\\n  - initial_mask_wearing: boolean; initial behavioral state at t0.\\n  - family_connections, work_school_connections, community_connections: integers; desired degree per social layer.\\n  - total_connections: integer; typically equals the sum of layer-specific connections (sanity check).\\n\\n- Relationships / structure:\\n  - No explicit edges; connection fields are counts (degree sequence) per layer, implying a multiplex (family, work/school, community) network.\\n  - total_connections aggregates the three layer counts; verify consistency per row.\\n  - Implicit constraints:\\n    - Students and school-aged agents should have work_school layer ties; retirees may have zero there.\\n    - Family layer spans across age groups; community layer is general.\\n\\n- Use in simulation design:\\n  - Initialize agent nodes with attributes: demographics, risk_perception, and initial_mask_wearing.\\n  - Generate three-layer social network using the per-layer degree sequences (e.g., configuration model per layer), then combine into a multiplex/weighted network.\\n  - Use layer types to weight influence (e.g., stronger influence within family; medium at work/school; variable in community).\\n  - Behavior update rules:\\n    - Social influence depends on mask adoption among neighbors (possibly per-layer weighted).\\n    - Individual susceptibility/threshold modulated by risk_perception, age_group, and occupation (e.g., students influenced more by school peers).\\n  - Validation/checks:\\n    - Ensure nonnegative integer degrees; total_connections \\u2248 sum of layer degrees.\\n    - Handle agents with zero degree (isolates).\\n    - Keep risk_perception within [0,1] and initial_mask_wearing as boolean.",\n    "Concise semantic metadata summary for social_network.json\\n\\n- Overall data structure and type\\n  - JSON object representing a static, multiplex (multi-layer) social network.\\n  - Top-level: mapping from agent IDs (as strings) to their neighborhood lists by layer.\\n  - Layer lists are arrays of integer agent IDs (neighbors).\\n\\n- Meaning of keys or columns\\n  - \\"<agent_id>\\": a unique agent/node identifier (stringified; neighbors are ints).\\n  - family: close-tie household/family connections for the agent.\\n  - work_school: coworkers/classmates connections.\\n  - community: broader community/social contacts.\\n  - all: the union of family \\u222a work_school \\u222a community for the agent (appears de-duplicated).\\n\\n- Relationships or nested elements\\n  - Each agent maps to an object containing four arrays; these arrays are neighbor lists for that agent on distinct layers.\\n  - The network is likely undirected in practice, but directionality is not enforced; reciprocity should be validated if needed.\\n  - Layers are disjoint categories but may overlap in members; \\u201call\\u201d prevents double counting only within an agent\\u2019s list.\\n  - IDs appear to span 0\\u2013999 (1000 agents); keys are strings while neighbor IDs are integers\\u2014normalize types when indexing.\\n  - Edges are unweighted in the file; no timestamps\\u2014this is a static snapshot; no self-loops observed.\\n\\n- How this data should inform simulation entities or interactions\\n  - Agents: one agent per top-level key; augment with behavioral state (e.g., wears_mask: True/False), susceptibility to influence, thresholds, and compliance propensity\\u2014these are not provided.\\n  - Interaction topology: use the three layers as distinct influence channels with layer-specific contact frequencies or influence weights (e.g., family > work_school > community).\\n  - Update dynamics: at each timestep, an agent\\u2019s mask-wearing state can be updated based on neighbors\\u2019 states aggregated by layer (e.g., weighted fraction/threshold, contagion probability).\\n  - Scheduling:\\n    - Layered interactions: sample contacts per layer with different probabilities to reflect tie strength.\\n    - Or build an aggregated graph from \\u201call\\u201d with weights derived from layer membership to avoid double counting.\\n  - Seeding and interventions: select initial adopters by layer (e.g., seed families or workplaces) and simulate policies (e.g., workplace mandates affecting the work_school layer only).\\n  - Data handling cautions:\\n    - Convert keys to ints or neighbor IDs to strings for consistent lookups.\\n    - If treating the network as undirected, optionally symmetrize edges.\\n    - Ensure no duplicate neighbor influence when combining layers; either use \\u201call\\u201d once with weights or keep layers separate.",\n    "- Overall structure and type:\\n  - CSV in long/panel format: one row per (day, agent) observation.\\n  - Likely primary key: (day, agent_id). No nesting; flat schema.\\n\\n- Columns and meanings:\\n  - day (int): discrete time step (t = 0,1,2,\\u2026) for the simulation.\\n  - agent_id (int): unique identifier of an agent; repeats across days.\\n  - wearing_mask (bool): agent\\u2019s behavioral state at day t.\\n  - received_info (bool): whether the agent was exposed to information (e.g., message/campaign) on day t.\\n\\n- Relationships/nested elements:\\n  - Temporal dependency within agent_id across consecutive days; suitable for Markov/transition modeling.\\n  - No explicit social network edges or group labels present; network must be provided elsewhere or synthesized.\\n  - No nested structures; relationships are inferred via time and repeated agent_id.\\n\\n- How this informs simulation entities/interactions:\\n  - Agents: instantiate one agent per unique agent_id with state variables wearing_mask and received_info that update each tick (day).\\n  - Initialization: use day 0 rows to set initial mask-wearing states (and initial info exposure if present).\\n  - Dynamics/parameters:\\n    - Estimate transition probabilities P(wearing_mask_t | wearing_mask_{t-1}, received_info_t, \\u2026) from the panel to calibrate behavior adoption, persistence, and relapse.\\n    - Model the effect of received_info as an exogenous shock increasing adoption probability and/or persistence.\\n    - If adding social contagion, combine calibrated baseline transitions with peer influence from the network (e.g., function of neighbors\\u2019 mask rate), since network structure is not in this file.\\n  - Validation: compare simulated time series of wearing_mask per agent or aggregate adoption curves against the empirical panel to assess fit.\\n  - Data handling notes: parse booleans correctly; ensure continuity of day indices per agent; handle agents/days not observed (missing rows) explicitly in the simulation setup."\n  ]\n}\n\nPlease design a simulation model that addresses the task requirements. Your response should include:\n\n1. The most appropriate modeling approach (agent-based, system dynamics, network-based, etc.)\n2. The structure of the simulation (entities, behaviors, interactions)\n3. The environment representation (grid, continuous space, network, etc.)\n4. Necessary parameters and configuration options\n5. Initialization procedures\n6. The algorithms to implement key behaviors and interactions\n7. A plan for how the code should be structured\n8. Any prediction or evaluation period specified by the task (e.g., days 30-39), and include it as a `prediction_period` field in the model plan.\n9. The evaluation metrics required by the task, and include them as an `evaluation_metrics` list in the model plan.\n\nResponse Format:\nPlease structure your response as a JSON object with the following structure:\n{\n  "model_type": "agent_based | system_dynamics | network | etc.",\n  "description": "Brief description of the model approach",\n  "entities": [\n    {\n      "name": "Entity name",\n      "attributes": ["attribute1", "attribute2", ...],\n      "behaviors": ["behavior1", "behavior2", ...],\n      "initialization": {\n        "method": "random |